## A new project: init with no flags

$ handoff init
Created <scratch>/new/.handoff. Git ignores it; nothing else in the project changed.
Roles: implementer, reviewer and reviewer-2; reviewer and reviewer-2 review.
Main branch: main.

To keep each CLI's sessions on duty here, run these and do what they say:
  handoff settings claude
  handoff settings codex
Then give each session its role: tell it, such as "you review", and it runs handoff role <role>.
# exit: 0

$ handoff settings codex
Put these two files in this project's .codex/ folder, merged with any already
there, and keep it out of git (add .codex/ to .git/info/exclude). Codex reads
them only in a project it trusts. The next time Codex starts here, it asks you
to review the new hooks: choose Trust all.

Every Codex session in this project then takes the role you give it:
tell it, and it runs handoff role <role>. Until then it has none. Without a role, handoff on its own never shows it your decisions.
It gets the brief when its first turn starts and after a summary. When its turn
would end, a hook listens for its work and continues it with what arrives. And
handoff runs outside Codex's sandbox, so its notifications and pop-ups reach
you; handoff decide is forbidden.

.codex/hooks.json:
{
  "hooks": {
    "SessionStart": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          {
            "command": "exec handoff hook brief",
            "timeout": 30,
            "type": "command"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "Stop": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          {
            "command": "exec handoff hook listen",
            "timeout": 86400,
            "type": "command"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}

.codex/rules/handoff.rules:
# handoff writes only under .handoff/ and has no network code (proposal §9).
prefix_rule(pattern=["handoff"], decision="allow", justification="handoff runs outside the sandbox so that it can notify the operator")
prefix_rule(pattern=["handoff", "decide"], decision="forbidden", justification="The operator decides, not an agent")
# exit: 0

## A project made with roles named after the agents, as the operator's was, and settings from before H16 that pass --prefer
$ handoff init --roles claude,codex

# Claude Code on Opus starts; its hook passes --prefer claude:
handoff: old hands work between agents through handoff, and this session has no role in it. When the operator gives you one, run `handoff role <role>` (the roles are claude, codex); it prints your brief. Until then, leave handoff alone.
# Codex starts:
handoff: old hands work between agents through handoff, and this session has no role in it. When the operator gives you one, run `handoff role <role>` (the roles are claude, codex); it prints your brief. Until then, leave handoff alone.
# sessions with a role: []

$ handoff roles add implementer reviewer,reviewer-2
Added implementer, reviewer and reviewer-2.
Roles: claude, codex, implementer, reviewer and reviewer-2; claude, codex, reviewer and reviewer-2 review.
Give a session a role by telling it; it runs handoff role <role>.
# exit: 0

# The operator gives each session its role:
# in Claude Code on Opus:
$ handoff role implementer
You are now implementer in old.
# in Claude Code on Fable:
$ handoff role reviewer
You are now reviewer in old.
# in Codex:
$ handoff role reviewer-2
You are now reviewer-2 in old.
# sessions with a role: [s-fable.json s-opus.json t-codex.json ]

